--- body ---
## Context

Fifth round of live findings, testing v0.3.20. Two more genuine
correctness bugs in `getSDTCoverage()`, both found by the author watching
real numbers on a real, actively-syncing 16 606-attachment library.

## Findings and fixes

1. **"0 attachments in the library" on boot is false.** `state.total`
   starts at its initial 0 before `host.list()` has ever resolved — not a
   measurement of an empty library, the absence of one. The diagnostics
   line now stays hidden until a real total is established
   (`state.total > 0 || state.censusSnapshot !== null`), matching the
   discipline `describeSDTCensusAccount` already applies to its own rows.

2. **Switching off showed a different, plausible-looking wrong number
   every time.** Root cause: `getSDTCoverage()` decided whether to trust
   the live counts by checking `state.phase === 'census'` — but
   `disarmSDTSitter()` overwrites `phase` to `'switched-off'` the instant
   the switch is thrown, whatever the sitter's own periodic re-census
   (every ~30 s, from empty, independent of the switch) was doing
   underneath. Freezing on the live counts unconditionally (ticket 0747)
   was right if that walk had finished, wrong if the click landed
   mid-rebuild: the reader froze on a half-reclassified count, different
   and "in the high range" depending on exactly how far the interrupted
   walk had got.

   Fixed by replacing the phase check with the actual signal that
   matters: `state.scanned === state.total` — true only when the walk
   that most recently touched `state.counts` genuinely finished, whatever
   phase claims to be current. Falls back to the last known-complete
   snapshot (`state.censusSnapshot`, from ticket 0751) whenever the live
   counts are mid-rebuild, regardless of phase.

Not fixed this round, and reported to the author rather than guessed at:
the denominator (library size minus out-of-scope items) can still bounce
between *completed* censuses if per-item classification itself changes —
traced to a "sniff vs. declared format" check that can behave
differently while the library's own sync is still settling metadata, but
not conclusively isolated. Author's own call: hold for further
investigation rather than paper over it with a display-level debounce.

## Test

- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`: a new integration test drives a real
  completed sweep, then a second sweep interrupted mid-rebuild, throws
  the switch (reproducing `disarmSDTSitter()`'s phase overwrite by hand,
  since this test drives the scheduler directly), and asserts coverage
  reads the last complete count, not the interrupted rebuild — verified
  red against the old `phase === 'census'` check.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs`, `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`: full
  suites re-run unmodified and pass, confirming the rewrite is
  behaviorally compatible with every previously-verified scenario.

## Verification

- [x] All four `.mjs` suites pass
- [x] `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py`, `tests/test_sitter_host_apis.py` pass
- [x] `make check-fast` — 1313 passed, 15 skipped, no failures
- [x] Manifest/update.json bumped 0.3.20 -> 0.3.21, version guard clean
- [x] The mid-rebuild fix verified with a positive control (red against
      the old `phase === 'census'` check)

## Exit criteria

The diagnostics line never states a total before one is known. Coverage
never freezes on a walk that was still rebuilding when interrupted,
regardless of what phase the interruption leaves behind.
